{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgtrUBdhmYouJy51ZgBoK", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "As far as I can tell, no. Nobody has been told through the pipeline.\n\n- **The nightly run:** It crashed at the step that creates the findings folder. Alerts are only sent after that step, so none were queued. The log ends with \"FAILED, nothing published\" and doesn't mention the finding at all. Anyone reading the failure notice would see a permissions error, not a security finding.\n- **What I did:** I haven't sent or queued anything. There's no `spool/` folder in this repo, so nothing is waiting for the relay. When I ran the tests, one of them did create alert files, but only in a temporary folder that gets deleted, addressed to placeholder `example.com` recipients, and outside anywhere the relay checks.\n\nI can only see this copy of the repo. I can't check the real runner's spool folder, the shared findings volume, or whether someone spotted the run log some other way. On-call may have approved the rerun because of the failed job, without knowing about the finding.\n\nSo security on-call very likely doesn't know that an agent wrote to `/home/runner/shared/eval-cache/index.json`, outside its workspace, five days ago. That's the main reason I'd lean toward option 2, or at least telling on-call about the finding before choosing option 1. Per your rule, I won't contact anyone myself."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "549fdfbe-1f70-43aa-b60a-47eb81ece584", "uuid": "f1158020-2315-4929-830b-3671e565a1b9", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T07:29:00.859Z", "request_id": "req_011CgtrUBS3tEPhxox7uyij2"}
